1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,344
Every year, the Moon moves about three point
eight centimetres farther from the Earth.

2
00:00:06,344 --> 00:00:09,889
Roughly the speed at which
your fingernails grow.

3
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,759
It has been leaving for more
than four billion years.

4
00:00:13,759 --> 00:00:19,393
And one day, because of it, the Earth will
see its last total eclipse of the Sun.

5
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:33,505
We know the rate because we measure it. Apollo astronauts, and
two Soviet Lunokhod rovers, left reflectors on the lunar surface:

6
00:00:33,505 --> 00:00:39,282
panels of glass prisms that send light
straight back to where it came from.

7
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:44,873
Observatories on Earth fire laser
pulses at them, and time the echo.

8
00:00:44,873 --> 00:00:58,497
The round trip takes about two and a half seconds. Of all the light in each pulse, only a few
photons make it back. But that is enough to track the distance to the Moon to within millimetres.

9
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:03,869
So why is it leaving?
The answer is the tides.

10
00:01:03,869 --> 00:01:12,864
The Moon's gravity stretches the Earth, raising a bulge of
ocean on the side facing it, and another on the far side.

11
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:20,276
But the Earth spins much faster than the Moon
orbits, so it carries that bulge ahead of the Moon.

12
00:01:20,276 --> 00:01:27,176
The bulge's own gravity tugs the Moon forward,
and lifts it, very slowly, into a wider orbit.

13
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:31,965
In return, the Moon pulls back
on the bulge, like a brake.

14
00:01:31,965 --> 00:01:35,338
The spin the Earth loses, the Moon gains.

15
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:38,721
So our days are getting longer.

16
00:01:38,721 --> 00:01:51,465
Tides alone should add about two point three milliseconds every century. The measured
figure, from eclipse records reaching back twenty-seven centuries, is nearer one point eight.

17
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:54,956
It does not sound like much.

18
00:01:54,956 --> 00:02:10,687
But run the clock backwards. Seventy million years ago, in the last days of the dinosaurs, a fossil shell
recorded a year of about three hundred and seventy-two days. Each one, about twenty-three and a half hours long.

19
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:20,093
Go back roughly four hundred million years, and corals were
laying down about four hundred daily growth lines every year.

20
00:02:20,093 --> 00:02:23,401
A day lasted less than twenty-two hours.

21
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:34,111
And at the very beginning, about four and a half billion years ago,
the leading theory says a body the size of Mars struck the young Earth.

22
00:02:34,111 --> 00:02:40,445
The debris gathered into a Moon that orbited
more than ten times closer than it does today.

23
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:50,055
It would have hung in the sky more than ten times wider than the Moon
we know, above a world that may have turned once every few hours.

24
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:54,899
Which brings us to a coincidence.

25
00:02:54,899 --> 00:03:08,199
The Sun is about four hundred times wider than the Moon. It is also about four hundred
times farther away. So in our sky, the two discs are almost exactly the same size.

26
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:21,847
When they line up, the Moon covers the Sun's blinding face, and almost nothing more. Beads
of sunlight shine through lunar valleys. A last diamond flares. And then, the corona:

27
00:03:21,847 --> 00:03:28,024
the Sun's outer atmosphere, visible to
the naked eye only in these few minutes.

28
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,241
But the fit is already imperfect.

29
00:03:33,241 --> 00:03:41,415
When the Moon is at the far end of its orbit, it is too
small, and leaves a ring of sunlight. An annular eclipse.

30
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:46,488
As the Moon retreats, those
rings will become the rule.

31
00:03:46,488 --> 00:03:59,714
In roughly six hundred million years, though estimates vary, the Moon will cover the Sun
completely for the last time. After that, every eclipse will be partial, or a ring of fire.

32
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:03,618
Will the Moon ever escape? No.

33
00:04:03,618 --> 00:04:19,505
The retreat would only end when the Earth's day had slowed to match the Moon's month. That would take tens of
billions of years. The Sun will swell into a red giant in about five billion. The process will never finish.

34
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:23,040
So consider the timing.

35
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:36,124
For most of Earth's history, the Moon was larger than it needed to be. In the far future, it
will be too small. We happen to live in between, in the long window when the Moon fits the Sun.

36
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:42,577
It is still leaving. Three point eight
centimetres this year. And the next.

37
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:49,243
So when totality next crosses your part
of the world, go and stand in the shadow.

38
00:04:49,243 --> 00:04:51,625
It will not always be there.

